\section{Executive summary}

\paragraph{Overall posture.} The SE keeps its main promise. Reading pad material out of the chip needs both the enrolled owner key (the TLS peer SPKI pin) and the PIN (TROPIC01 MAC-and-Destroy). A compromised NonSecure image, an arbitrary USB host, or a SAE does not have both. The weaker points are elsewhere:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The \textbf{inter-SAE RMI service} is designed for a network that the deployment treats as untrusted and world-wide. The listener binds all interfaces. Any certificate under the SAE root CA can call every method, and the caller's SAE identity is taken from method arguments rather than from the TLS peer (\fid{F-01}, \fid{F-02}, \fid{F-06}).
\item On the SE, \textbf{\code{CREDS SAE}} lets anyone who holds the owner key replace the SAE CA that \code{PROVISION} trusts. That person can then act as a SAE towards this device and inject pads they know. It does \emph{not} give them \code{ENCRYPT}/\code{DECRYPT}, which still needs the PIN (\fid{F-03}).
\item There are several smaller inconsistencies between the documentation and the code (\fid{F-14}).
\end{enumerate}

