\section{Findings}\label{sec:findings}
Each finding gives its context, the attacker model, the evidence (path, function, and lines), the attack sequence, the impact, the scores, and a pointer to its mitigation.

\subsection*{F-01: RMI methods trust caller-supplied SAE identity \hfill \sev{High}}
\textbf{Context:} production SAE; untrusted WAN. \textbf{Model:} A, with any certificate under \code{root-ca}: a compromised or rogue peer SAE, or a mis-issued leaf.

\textbf{Evidence.} \code{NodeCommandsService} implements the 8 remote methods, and none of them reads the TLS peer identity. A search for \code{getPeerCertificates} or \code{getClientHost} on the RMI path finds nothing.
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{removeRecord(h1,h2)} calls \code{atomicRecordStateMap.forceDelete} with no ownership check (lines 146--154).
\item \code{deleteShare(h1,h2)} is \emph{exposed on the remote interface}. A remote caller makes the callee delete its counterpart on the SAE named in the row, and then its own row (lines 160--173).
\item \code{markOriginProvisioned} promotes \code{RECORD_SHARED} to \code{ORIGIN_PROVISIONED} with no issuer check (lines 79--82).
\item \code{synchronize}, \code{lockFetch}, and \code{insert} validate the reservation against the \code{issuingSaeId} \emph{argument}, not against the caller. \code{insert} then fetches from the KME with \code{getKeyWithKeyIds(issuingSaeId, keyIds)} and stores the result (lines 89--143).
\item \code{getRecordMetadata} returns the state, the SAE IDs, and the pad length for any hash pair.
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Attack sequence.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Obtain any \code{root-ca} leaf, or compromise one SAE.
\item Connect to a victim SAE's registry.
\item Enumerate pairs with \code{getRecordMetadata}, or destroy shares on two SAEs at once with \code{removeRecord} or \code{deleteShare}.
\item Alternatively, run \code{synchronize}, \code{lockFetch}, and \code{insert} with \code{issuingSaeId} set to a third SAE.
\item Or call \code{markOriginProvisioned} to unlock the re-delivery logic.
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Impact.} Integrity and availability of pad distribution: clients can end up with mismatched shares, or a share is deleted after one side was provisioned. No pad plaintext is returned over RMI; the methods return only booleans and metadata. One compromised SAE can act \emph{as every other SAE}.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:H/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:H/A:H} $=$ \textbf{6.7}. \code{CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:H/UI:N/VC:L/VI:H/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N} $=$ \textbf{7.1}. STRIDE: S, T, D. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-1, M-2.

\subsection*{F-02: RMI listens on all interfaces; no peer pinning \hfill \sev{Medium}}
\textbf{Context:} production SAE. \textbf{Model:} A.

\textbf{Evidence.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{RmiManager.PqcTlsRmiServerSocketFactory.createServerSocket} calls the 4-argument \code{SoftwareTls.createServerSocket}, which passes \code{bindAddress = null}. That is the wildcard address.
\item This applies to the exported object (port \code{40000 + NODE_RMI_PORT}) and to \code{LocateRegistry.createRegistry} (port \code{NODE_RMI_PORT}).
\item \code{NODE_HOSTNAME} only sets \code{java.rmi.server.hostname}, which is the advertised name, not the bind address.
\item Any \code{root-ca} leaf is accepted. \code{sae-nodes.json} maps SAE IDs to addresses for outgoing calls, but is not used to authenticate incoming calls.
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Impact.} Every NIC exposes the expensive ML-DSA/ML-KEM handshake to the Internet. Unauthenticated handshake floods are a DoS vector, and the open listener maximises the reach of \fid{F-01} and \fid{F-06}.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L} $=$ \textbf{5.3}. \code{CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:N/VC:N/VI:N/VA:L/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N} $=$ \textbf{6.9}. STRIDE: E, D. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-2, M-3, and the parameter sheet.

\subsection*{F-03: \texttt{CREDS SAE} replaces the PROVISION trust anchor with the owner key only \hfill \sev{High}}
\textbf{Context:} locked SE; home PC compromise. \textbf{Model:} A. The attacker has stolen the owner key and its PKCS\#12 password, and has physical USB access to the SE.

\textbf{Evidence.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{se_manage_apply} accepts \code{SE_MANAGE_CREDS_SAE} only with \code{pin_len == 0}, so no PIN is involved.
\item \code{do_creds_sae} checks only the length (\code{se_manage.c} lines 252--264).
\item \code{se_creds_set_sae_ca} overwrites the CA and keeps the device certificate (\code{se_creds.c} lines 212--237). It does not compare with the previous CA, check \code{BasicConstraints}, or ask for confirmation.
\item The only authentication is the owner SPKI pin on the \code{MANAGE} TLS session.
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Attack sequence.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Steal \code{user.p12} and its password from the home PC.
\item Connect the victim SE and run \code{MANAGE CREDS SAE} with the attacker's CA.
\item Run \code{PROVISION} against an attacker SAE whose leaf chains to that CA, injecting pads the attacker knows.
\item The victim later encrypts with known keystream.
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{What the stolen owner key gives and does not give.}
\begin{itemize}
\item It gives the direct ability to \emph{spoof the SAE CA} on that device and \emph{inject pads}.
\item It allows the other \code{MANAGE} actions: \code{KEYGEN}, peer edits, \code{PAIRING}/\code{PAIRING LOAD}, and \code{INSERT SIGNED CSR}.
\item It does \textbf{not} allow \code{ENCRYPT} or \code{DECRYPT}. Those need the PIN, which TROPIC01 M\&D enforces; 7 failed tries destroy the KEK.
\item It does not grant RMI access, because the RMI truststore is \code{root-ca}, not the CA installed on the SE.
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Status.} \code{CREDS SAE} stays owner-only by product decision, so this is recorded as an accepted design risk. The acceptance assumes the owner key is password-protected and the device stays with its user.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:P/AC:L/PR:H/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:L} $=$ \textbf{5.7}. \code{CVSS:4.0/AV:P/AC:L/AT:N/PR:H/UI:P/VC:H/VI:H/VA:L/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N} $=$ \textbf{6.9}. STRIDE: S, T, E. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-4, optional hardening that keeps the command owner-only.

\subsection*{F-04: \texttt{OWNER SET} retry path installs the SAE CA without owner authentication \hfill \sev{Low}}
\textbf{Context:} SE after a failed enrollment. \textbf{Model:} A, physical USB.

\textbf{Evidence.} \code{se_auth_begin_owner} refuses only when \code{se_nv_has_owner()} \emph{and} \code{se_nv_has_device_sk()} are both true (\code{se_auth.c} lines 42--45). \code{run_owner_set} skips \code{se_owner_set} when an owner exists, but still runs \code{se_device_id_ensure} and \code{se_creds_set_sae_ca} with the CA from the \emph{unsigned} blob (lines 70--91).

\textbf{Attack sequence.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The owner SPKI is written, but ML-DSA keygen fails.
\item A host that is not the owner sends \code{OWNER SET} with its own CA.
\item The device keeps the legitimate owner but now trusts the attacker's SAE CA.
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Likelihood.} Low, because the owner notices a failed \code{INIT}.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:P/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N} $=$ \textbf{5.7}. \code{CVSS:4.0/AV:P/AC:L/AT:P/PR:N/UI:N/VC:H/VI:H/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N} $=$ \textbf{5.3}. STRIDE: S, T. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-5.

\subsection*{F-05: SE does not validate the peer chain on ENCRYPT/DECRYPT/MANAGE \hfill \sev{Low}}
\textbf{Evidence.} \code{tls_accept_peer_leaf} returns~1 and ignores \code{preverify}. Identity is the SPKI pin alone (\code{se_tls_user.c}). Validity period, issuer, and revocation of the owner certificate are never evaluated.

\textbf{Impact.} Revoking the owner key at the CA has no effect on the SE. A stolen owner key stays valid until \code{OWNER REPLACE}. This is by design, since pinning is stronger than CA validation. It is listed so that the manual revocation runbook does not assume otherwise.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:P/AC:H/PR:H/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N} $=$ \textbf{2.7}. CVSS:4.0 $=$ \textbf{1.0}. STRIDE: S. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-6.

\subsection*{F-06: RMI deserializes peer objects without an \texttt{ObjectInputFilter} \hfill \sev{Medium}}
\textbf{Context:} production SAE. \textbf{Model:} A, with a \code{root-ca} leaf.

\textbf{Evidence.} The remote methods take \code{Serializable} arguments (\code{ClientRecord}, \code{ClientHeader}, \code{Address}). Neither \code{jdk.serialFilter} nor an \code{ObjectInputFilter} is set anywhere in \code{JAVA_APPS}. The built-in JDK registry filter covers the registry only, not exported objects.

\textbf{Impact.} An authenticated peer can send arbitrary object graphs. If a usable gadget chain exists on the classpath, the result is code execution in the process that holds the HSM session. Otherwise it is a CPU or memory DoS.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:H/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H} $=$ \textbf{6.6}. \code{CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:H/AT:P/PR:H/UI:N/VC:H/VI:H/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N} $=$ \textbf{7.5}. STRIDE: T, E. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-7.

\subsection*{F-07: \texttt{PAIRING LOAD} is weaker than \texttt{PAIRING} \hfill \sev{Low}}
\textbf{Evidence.} \code{do_pairing_load} requires only an empty NV pairing record and a valid format (\code{se_manage.c} lines 325--349). It then loads a caller-supplied X25519 key and slot. By contrast, \code{PAIRING} generates the key on the device and burns SH0.

\textbf{Sequence.} With the owner key and \code{pairing-key.hex}, on an SE whose NV pairing record is empty, an attacker restores the old pairing and speaks L3 again. Combined with SPI access, this exposes R-MEM ciphertext. Pads still need \code{secure_dwk}, \code{fill_id}, and the PIN.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:P/AC:H/PR:H/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N} $=$ \textbf{2.7}. CVSS:4.0 $=$ \textbf{1.0}. STRIDE: E. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-8.

\subsection*{F-08: Factory SH0 is the L3 key until \texttt{PAIRING} \hfill \sev{Info}}
\textbf{Evidence.} \code{libtropic_user_config.h} selects the published \code{lt_sh0priv_prod0} key. Before \code{PAIRING}, anyone with SPI access can open an L3 session. \textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:P/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N} $=$ 3.5; CVSS:4.0 $=$ 1.0. \textbf{Mitigation:} make \code{PAIRING} a mandatory step before issuing a device (parameter sheet).

\subsection*{F-09: Plaintext device secrets in flash page 22; HDP not used \hfill \sev{Info (residual at RDP 2)}}
\textbf{Evidence.} Page~22 holds \code{secure_dwk}, the ML-DSA-44 SK, and the pairing SK in plaintext (\code{se_nv.c}, \code{SECURITY.md}). \code{HDP1EN} is \code{0x0}. At RDP~2, reading them needs an invasive attack. With page 22 an attacker can impersonate the device in TLS. Pads still require TROPIC01 and the PIN.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:P/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N} $=$ \textbf{5.7}. CVSS:4.0 $=$ \textbf{5.3}. STRIDE: I. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-9.

\subsection*{F-10: Unauthenticated USB console discloses state \hfill \sev{Low}}
\textbf{Evidence.} The SE-L1 commands (\code{PEER LIST}, \code{CLIENT HASH}/\code{CSR}, \code{TROPIC PUB}, \code{KEM PUB}, \code{OTP STATUS}) answer any host. Together they reveal enrollment state, peer nicknames and hashes, and the remaining pad budget.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:P/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N} $=$ \textbf{2.4}. CVSS:4.0 $=$ \textbf{2.4}. STRIDE: I. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-10.

\subsection*{F-11: SAE--KME hop is CLASSICAL TLS \hfill \sev{Medium (model B)}}
\textbf{Evidence.} \code{Node.loadQkdClient} uses \code{SoftwareTls.TlsProfile.CLASSICAL}, and pad plaintext is carried over that link.

\textbf{Impact.} A model-B attacker who records the link and later breaks X25519 learns the pads. This does not break device \code{PROVISION} TLS.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:N/A:N} $=$ \textbf{6.1}. CVSS:4.0 $=$ \textbf{6.0}. STRIDE: I. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-11.

\subsection*{F-12: PKI hygiene \hfill \sev{Low}}
\textbf{Evidence (\code{CertGenerator}).}
\begin{itemize}
\item Node and device leaves have no \code{KeyUsage} or \code{ExtendedKeyUsage} (\code{issuePurePqcFromPublicKey}).
\item The CAs use serial~1 and a one-year validity, with no path-length constraint.
\item Both CA keys are created on one host under the same \code{pkcs12Password}.
\item The SE does not check that the \code{CREDS SAE} DER is a CA certificate.
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Impact.} Role separation rests on the issuer alone, and a single host compromise yields both CAs.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:H/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N} $=$ \textbf{3.3}. CVSS:4.0 $=$ \textbf{2.1}. STRIDE: S, E. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-12.

\subsection*{F-13: Secret prompt echoes on \texttt{System.in} without a console \hfill \sev{Low}}
\textbf{Evidence.} \code{EnvSecrets.scan} uses \code{Console.readPassword} when a console exists. Otherwise it reads \code{System.in} with echo (for example under a service manager or a pipe). This affects \code{HSM_PIN} and the QKD store passwords.

\textbf{Scores.} \code{CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N} $=$ \textbf{2.2}. CVSS:4.0 $=$ \textbf{1.0}. STRIDE: I. \textbf{Mitigation:} M-13.

\subsection*{F-14: Documentation contradicts code \hfill \sev{Info}}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The PIN budget is 7 tries in code, but 8 in \code{SECURITY.md}, \code{TROPIC.md}, \code{ACCESS_POINTS.md}, and \code{host/README.md}.
\item \code{SaeNode/README.md} calls \code{NODE_HOSTNAME} the ``RMI bind'' address (\fid{F-02}).
\item The \code{se_tls_client.c} header says encrypt/decrypt ``verify the client CA'' (\fid{F-05}).
\item The thesis says ``hybrid''; the code uses pure ML-KEM-768.
\item \code{ACCESS_POINTS.md} still says SaeNode is not in the tree.
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Mitigation:} correct the documents and the source comment.
\section{Mitigations}
Each mitigation names the asset, the boundary, the mechanism, and who can implement it.

\begin{description}[style=nextline,leftmargin=1.2em]
\item[M-1 RMI caller authorization (SAE code; F-01)]
\begin{itemize}
\item In every remote method, derive the caller's SAE ID from the TLS peer certificate. A custom \code{RMIServerSocketFactory} can record the verified peer leaf per connection and expose it to the service thread.
\item Map the certificate to a SAE ID through the pinned table (M-2).
\item Reject the call if \code{issuingSaeId} does not match the authenticated caller.
\item Allow \code{removeRecord} and \code{markOriginProvisioned} only from the counterpart SAE recorded in the row.
\item Remove \code{deleteShare} from the remote interface. It is a local operator action.
\item Restrict \code{getRecordMetadata} to rows where the caller is a participant.
\item Log the caller's ID on every deletion.
\end{itemize}
\item[M-2 RMI peer pinning (SAE code and configuration; F-01, F-02)] Add each peer's SPKI hash to \texttt{sae-nodes.json}. Accept an inbound handshake only from a listed leaf, in addition to the \texttt{root-ca} chain check. On outbound calls, check that the server leaf matches the SAE being addressed. Today any \texttt{root-ca} server leaf is accepted for any address.
\item[M-3 Bind address (SAE code; F-02)] Pass \texttt{InetAddress.getByName(NODE\_HOSTNAME)} to the 5-argument \texttt{SoftwareTls.createServerSocket} in \texttt{PqcTlsRmiServerSocketFactory}. This covers both the registry and the object port. Add a firewall allow-list and a per-source rate limit on TLS handshakes.
\item[M-4 Optional \texttt{CREDS SAE} hardening that keeps it owner-only (firmware; F-03)] Options, from cheapest:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item require the reset password, whose hash is already stored for \code{OWNER REPLACE};
\item require the PIN, so that the M\&D budget also limits this command;
\item check \code{BasicConstraints CA:true} on the new DER;
\item return the old and new CA fingerprints so that UserApp can ask for confirmation.
\end{enumerate}
Option (a) or (b) turns ``stolen owner key'' into ``stolen owner key plus a secret''.
\item[M-5 Close the \texttt{OWNER SET} retry (firmware; F-04)] Refuse in \texttt{se\_auth\_begin\_owner} whenever \texttt{se\_nv\_has\_owner()} is true. Alternatively, ignore the CA field on the retry path, so that the CA can then be set only through \texttt{MANAGE}.
\item[M-6 Revocation runbook (operator; F-05)] Revoking the owner at the CA does not reach the SE. For a lost PC or owner key, the runbook must say:
\begin{itemize}
\item run \code{OWNER REPLACE} with a new owner key;
\item treat any \code{CREDS SAE} performed since the theft as hostile;
\item revoke the device certificate under \code{client_ca}, which the SAE does check;
\item delete the peer counterpart.
\end{itemize}
Also fix the comment in \code{se_tls_client.c}.
\item[M-7 Serialization filter (SAE code; F-06)] Install a process-wide \texttt{ObjectInputFilter}, through \texttt{jdk.serialFilter} or programmatically in \texttt{Node} before \texttt{RmiManager.start}. It should allow only the \texttt{fel.cvut.node} record types, \texttt{java.lang} and \texttt{java.time} values, and \texttt{java.util} collections, and cap depth, array length, and total bytes.
\item[M-8 \texttt{PAIRING LOAD} (firmware; F-07)] Require the reset password or the PIN. Accept only a key whose public half matches the key TROPIC01 holds for that slot. Otherwise disable the command in production builds.
\item[M-9 Device secrets (firmware, long term; F-09)] The U535 has no SAES and no hardware unique key. Consider wrapping page~22 under a key held by TROPIC01 and released only through L3, so that a flash dump alone is not enough. Until then, RDP~2 is the control. The tree does not configure HDP, so no HDP ranges are proposed here.
\item[M-10 Console oracles (firmware; F-10)] Move \texttt{OTP STATUS} and \texttt{PEER LIST} behind \texttt{MANAGE}, or reduce the unauthenticated reply to a single ``enrolled'' flag.
\item[M-11 KME link (operations; F-11)] Run it on a private cable or segment. Move to \texttt{PURE\_PQC} once the KME supports it.
\item[M-12 PKI (CertGenerator, Node, SE; F-12)] Add \texttt{KeyUsage(digitalSignature)} and EKUs: \texttt{serverAuth} plus \texttt{clientAuth} for nodes, \texttt{clientAuth} for devices. Give the CAs \texttt{pathLen=0}. Use separate PKCS\#12 passwords, keep the CAs offline, and use random serials. Check EKUs in the Node trust managers.
\item[M-13 Prompt (SAE code; F-13)] Refuse to read a secret from a non-console \texttt{System.in}. Use an interactive console, or a \texttt{0600} file or descriptor.
\item[M-14 Fault hardening (firmware; residual)]
\begin{itemize}
\item Duplicate the critical decisions (owner pin, \code{has_owner}, cursor advance) with complementary checks.
\item Re-read the cursor in NV and in the mcounter after the advance and before the XOR.
\item Lock the GTZC and MPCBB configuration after init.
\item Configure MPCBB1 explicitly instead of relying on its reset default.
\end{itemize}
\end{description}

\subsection{Recommended CA and truststore architecture}\label{sec:mit-ca}
The SAE Node is the only component that needs both CAs: the client CA on the USB side and the SAE CA on the RMI side. The SE stores no client CA. It stores only the SAE CA (for \code{PROVISION}) and the owner pin (for USER sessions). The recommended state:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Two TLS contexts in the Node, never merged.}
  \begin{itemize}
  \item The USB device-server context trusts \code{client_ca} only.
  \item The RMI context trusts the SAE CA only.
  \end{itemize}
  This already holds in code (\code{createContextForCommandServer} and \code{createContextForNode}). Keep it as a tested invariant. A test should show that a \code{client_ca} leaf fails the RMI handshake and that a \code{root-ca} leaf fails the device handshake. No test currently asserts this.
\item \textbf{RMI peer pinning} on top of the CA check (M-2), so that even a mis-issued SAE-CA leaf is not accepted as a peer.
\item \textbf{Optional further split.} Today \code{root-ca} is both the RMI anchor and the CA the SE trusts in \code{PROVISION}. Separate ``SAE-RMI'' and ``SAE-provisioning'' CAs would limit the blast radius of one CA key.
\item \textbf{Role-binding extensions} (EKU, KeyUsage, path length), so that the separation does not rest on the issuer alone (M-12).
\item \textbf{Offline CA keys} with distinct passwords, kept off the SAE hosts. A production UserApp never holds \code{client_ca.p12}.
\end{enumerate}

